1
00:00:00,321 --> 00:00:05,028
In nineteen thirty-seven, one army ordered
a chocolate bar that tasted only a little

2
00:00:05,048 --> 00:00:06,773
better than a boiled potato.

3
00:00:07,741 --> 00:00:08,891
On purpose.

4
00:00:09,283 --> 00:00:13,518
Another army lost more soldiers to
hunger and disease than to enemy bullets.

5
00:00:13,774 --> 00:00:17,081
So which country fed its
soldiers best in World War Two?

6
00:00:17,931 --> 00:00:20,608
Today we rank six armies,
from starving to spoiled.

7
00:00:21,355 --> 00:00:23,103
And every army gets three scores.

8
00:00:23,813 --> 00:00:25,506
What the ration promised on paper.

9
00:00:26,218 --> 00:00:30,327
How it actually tasted. And the big one:
did the food ever reach the soldier?

10
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:34,530
Because a perfect menu means
nothing if the truck never arrives.

11
00:00:35,338 --> 00:00:38,169
Number six: the Imperial Japanese Army.

12
00:00:38,870 --> 00:00:43,525
By nineteen forty-two, Japan held a
huge empire across Asia and the Pacific.

13
00:00:43,545 --> 00:00:46,192
But it could not feed the men holding it.

14
00:00:46,892 --> 00:00:49,109
On paper, the Japanese soldier ate simply.

15
00:00:49,853 --> 00:00:53,988
His ration was mostly rice, with dried
fish, pickled plums and powdered soy

16
00:00:54,008 --> 00:00:55,158
paste.

17
00:00:55,408 --> 00:00:58,852
It was light, so a man could carry
several days of food on his back.

18
00:00:59,611 --> 00:01:01,137
But the plan had a fatal flaw.

19
00:01:01,829 --> 00:01:05,699
Japanese commanders often expected
troops to find food where they fought.

20
00:01:06,552 --> 00:01:10,705
So when American submarines sank the
supply ships, whole islands ran out of

21
00:01:10,725 --> 00:01:11,875
food.

22
00:01:12,035 --> 00:01:15,670
On Guadalcanal, Japanese soldiers
gave the island a new name.

23
00:01:15,690 --> 00:01:17,798
They called it Starvation Island.

24
00:01:18,505 --> 00:01:22,470
On New Guinea, cut-off units survived
on grass, roots and anything they could

25
00:01:22,490 --> 00:01:23,640
catch.

26
00:01:23,874 --> 00:01:26,768
Imagine a nineteen-year-old
private on a jungle island.

27
00:01:27,533 --> 00:01:29,067
His rice bag is empty.

28
00:01:29,087 --> 00:01:31,414
The next supply ship is
at the bottom of the sea.

29
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:33,370
And nobody is coming.

30
00:01:34,086 --> 00:01:38,745
Japanese historian Yoshida Yutaka
estimates that about sixty percent of

31
00:01:38,765 --> 00:01:41,557
Japan's military dead
never died in battle.

32
00:01:41,682 --> 00:01:44,345
They died from starvation and disease.

33
00:01:45,002 --> 00:01:46,366
So Japan finishes last.

34
00:01:46,397 --> 00:01:51,353
Not because the rice was bad, but because
for over a million men, it never came.

35
00:01:52,311 --> 00:01:55,649
But hunger was not the only
way to ruin a soldier's dinner.

36
00:01:55,669 --> 00:01:58,841
Some armies had food, and
their soldiers still hated it.

37
00:01:59,722 --> 00:02:01,823
Number five: Italy.

38
00:02:02,535 --> 00:02:07,200
Italian soldiers in North Africa lived on
a hard biscuit called the galletta, and

39
00:02:07,220 --> 00:02:08,839
small tins of boiled beef.

40
00:02:09,883 --> 00:02:14,294
The tins were stamped with two letters,
A and M, for the Military Administration.

41
00:02:15,208 --> 00:02:16,663
But soldiers had their own meaning.

42
00:02:17,361 --> 00:02:20,273
A popular story says they
read it as Asino Morto.

43
00:02:20,413 --> 00:02:21,563
Dead donkey.

44
00:02:22,093 --> 00:02:25,138
And when German troops ate the
same tins, they had a joke too.

45
00:02:25,921 --> 00:02:27,174
Alter Mann. Old man.

46
00:02:27,887 --> 00:02:31,987
So in the desert heat, the same beef and
the same biscuits came back, day after

47
00:02:32,007 --> 00:02:33,157
day.

48
00:02:33,275 --> 00:02:36,990
And the Italian army had far fewer
trucks than the British it was fighting.

49
00:02:37,804 --> 00:02:40,025
So food often moved
slowly, if it moved at all.

50
00:02:40,843 --> 00:02:43,591
But Italy had one comfort
that most armies did not.

51
00:02:43,856 --> 00:02:47,041
Its soldiers were entitled
to a daily ration of wine.

52
00:02:47,865 --> 00:02:50,093
That small glass lifts Italy above Japan.

53
00:02:50,256 --> 00:02:54,408
But a glass of wine was nothing
compared to what the next army poured.

54
00:02:55,273 --> 00:02:57,745
Number four: the Soviet Red Army.

55
00:02:58,462 --> 00:03:00,147
Its ration was built on one thing.

56
00:03:00,887 --> 00:03:02,037
Bread.

57
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:06,262
From September nineteen forty-one, a
front-line soldier was promised eight

58
00:03:06,282 --> 00:03:08,165
hundred grams of rye bread a day.

59
00:03:09,116 --> 00:03:10,408
In winter, nine hundred.

60
00:03:11,099 --> 00:03:14,490
That is almost a kilo of heavy
black bread, every single day.

61
00:03:14,600 --> 00:03:18,618
Bread came first because it was cheap,
filling, and could be baked almost

62
00:03:18,638 --> 00:03:19,788
anywhere.

63
00:03:20,261 --> 00:03:23,575
On the march, it became hard dried
crusts that lasted for weeks.

64
00:03:24,310 --> 00:03:28,123
A soldier soaked them in tea or soup,
or just chewed for a very long time.

65
00:03:29,012 --> 00:03:31,320
But the Red Army also had a famous extra.

66
00:03:31,471 --> 00:03:35,285
Front-line troops were issued
one hundred grams of vodka a day.

67
00:03:36,161 --> 00:03:39,645
But the biggest surprise in Soviet
mess tins came from America.

68
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:44,447
Through Lend-Lease, the United States
shipped huge amounts of canned meat to the

69
00:03:44,467 --> 00:03:45,617
Soviet Union.

70
00:03:46,225 --> 00:03:49,068
Soldiers gave the American
cans a sarcastic nickname.

71
00:03:49,218 --> 00:03:51,276
They called them the second front.

72
00:03:51,981 --> 00:03:56,433
Because for years, American cans reached
Russia long before American soldiers

73
00:03:56,453 --> 00:03:57,603
landed in France.

74
00:03:58,475 --> 00:04:00,028
So the Red Army sits in the middle.

75
00:04:00,718 --> 00:04:03,713
Plenty of bread, a little vodka,
and a lot of borrowed meat.

76
00:04:04,521 --> 00:04:08,456
But its enemy had a ration so precious
that soldiers could be punished just for

77
00:04:08,476 --> 00:04:09,626
eating it.

78
00:04:10,103 --> 00:04:13,022
Number three: the
Wehrmacht, the German army.

79
00:04:13,734 --> 00:04:17,740
Every German soldier carried an
emergency ration called the iron portion.

80
00:04:18,636 --> 00:04:22,052
Hard biscuits, a tin of meat,
dried vegetables, coffee and salt.

81
00:04:22,933 --> 00:04:25,180
He could only eat it when
his commander allowed it.

82
00:04:25,864 --> 00:04:27,904
Men who opened it early
could be locked up.

83
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:32,015
Day to day, the army cooked hot
meals in mobile field kitchens.

84
00:04:32,254 --> 00:04:34,736
Soldiers nicknamed them goulash cannons.

85
00:04:35,442 --> 00:04:37,687
In the good years, this
system worked very well.

86
00:04:38,419 --> 00:04:42,205
But it depended on trucks, trains and
horses, and Russia broke all three.

87
00:04:43,093 --> 00:04:47,487
At Stalingrad, the trapped Sixth Army ran
so short of food that it slaughtered its

88
00:04:47,507 --> 00:04:48,657
own horses.

89
00:04:49,184 --> 00:04:53,367
For many German soldiers, Christmas
nineteen forty-two meant snow, frozen

90
00:04:53,387 --> 00:04:56,018
fingers and a thin soup
made from horsemeat.

91
00:04:57,132 --> 00:04:58,660
So Germany takes third place.

92
00:04:58,726 --> 00:05:01,902
A brilliant plan that collapsed
when the roads ran out.

93
00:05:02,698 --> 00:05:05,061
But the next army solved
the problem differently.

94
00:05:05,799 --> 00:05:08,406
It packed a whole day of
food into one wooden box.

95
00:05:09,195 --> 00:05:11,597
Number two: the British Army.

96
00:05:12,309 --> 00:05:14,517
Its secret weapon was
the Composite Ration.

97
00:05:15,229 --> 00:05:16,800
Soldiers just called it compo.

98
00:05:17,486 --> 00:05:19,605
One crate fed fourteen
men for a whole day.

99
00:05:20,421 --> 00:05:24,668
Inside were tins of steak and kidney
pudding, Scotch broth, bacon, vegetables

100
00:05:24,688 --> 00:05:25,922
and a sweet pudding.

101
00:05:26,890 --> 00:05:29,465
There were biscuits, jam,
chocolate and cigarettes too.

102
00:05:30,308 --> 00:05:32,607
But the most British thing
in the box was the tea.

103
00:05:33,333 --> 00:05:35,713
It came already mixed with
sugar and milk powder.

104
00:05:36,545 --> 00:05:40,150
So a soldier only needed hot
water to brew up, almost anywhere.

105
00:05:41,065 --> 00:05:45,623
In the desert, in the jungle or in a muddy
Normandy hedgerow, a hot brew was the

106
00:05:45,643 --> 00:05:47,861
moment a soldier felt human again.

107
00:05:48,942 --> 00:05:51,603
The crates came in different
menus marked with letters.

108
00:05:52,343 --> 00:05:54,694
But men often got the
same menu again and again.

109
00:05:55,563 --> 00:05:59,201
Still, Britain earns second place
for comfort, variety and that tea.

110
00:06:00,059 --> 00:06:02,273
But the winner had something
the British did not.

111
00:06:02,989 --> 00:06:05,783
Money, factories, and a very
strange idea of dessert.

112
00:06:06,591 --> 00:06:08,913
Number one: the United States.

113
00:06:09,630 --> 00:06:11,669
Remember that chocolate
bar from the start?

114
00:06:12,363 --> 00:06:17,037
In nineteen thirty-seven, an army officer
named Paul Logan asked Hershey to make it.

115
00:06:18,068 --> 00:06:21,236
He wanted it to taste only a
little better than a boiled potato.

116
00:06:22,050 --> 00:06:25,670
Because if it tasted good, soldiers
would eat it before the emergency.

117
00:06:26,518 --> 00:06:30,912
So the D ration bar was hard, bitter and
so dense that men struggled to bite it.

118
00:06:31,917 --> 00:06:33,783
The daily rations were not loved either.

119
00:06:34,490 --> 00:06:37,141
Soldiers grumbled about the
same canned hash and stew.

120
00:06:37,988 --> 00:06:39,495
So why is America number one?

121
00:06:39,604 --> 00:06:42,818
Because it could deliver food
almost anywhere on Earth.

122
00:06:43,612 --> 00:06:48,284
A small K ration box held a full meal,
with coffee, candy, gum and cigarettes.

123
00:06:49,186 --> 00:06:51,496
For small groups, there
was the ten-in-one ration.

124
00:06:52,225 --> 00:06:54,170
One box fed ten men for a whole day.

125
00:06:54,950 --> 00:06:59,521
Behind the lines, field kitchens served
hot meals, and on Thanksgiving, many units

126
00:06:59,541 --> 00:07:00,691
even got turkey.

127
00:07:01,611 --> 00:07:03,109
But the real proof was dessert.

128
00:07:03,790 --> 00:07:08,715
In nineteen forty-five, the United States
Navy spent about one million dollars on a

129
00:07:08,735 --> 00:07:10,557
concrete barge with one job.

130
00:07:11,581 --> 00:07:15,215
It made ice cream. About ten
gallons every seven minutes.

131
00:07:15,924 --> 00:07:19,646
It was towed to the Pacific to serve
sailors on ships without their own ice

132
00:07:19,666 --> 00:07:20,816
cream machines.

133
00:07:21,527 --> 00:07:26,058
So the country that made chocolate taste
bad on purpose also floated an ice cream

134
00:07:26,078 --> 00:07:27,587
factory across an ocean.

135
00:07:28,565 --> 00:07:30,627
That is the gap between
starving and spoiled.

136
00:07:31,337 --> 00:07:33,569
On one island, a soldier ate grass.

137
00:07:33,657 --> 00:07:36,396
Across the same ocean,
a sailor ate ice cream.

138
00:07:37,289 --> 00:07:38,818
And the difference was not taste.

139
00:07:39,463 --> 00:07:41,140
It was ships, trucks and factories.

140
00:07:41,849 --> 00:07:43,368
So here is the final ranking.

141
00:07:44,057 --> 00:07:48,726
Japan, Italy, the Soviet Union, Germany,
Britain, and at the very top, the United

142
00:07:48,746 --> 00:07:49,896
States.

143
00:07:50,298 --> 00:07:51,750
But food was only half the deal.

144
00:07:52,427 --> 00:07:57,026
Next time, we rank how much every World
War Two army actually paid its soldiers.

145
00:07:57,182 --> 00:07:59,247
Subscribe, so you don't miss it.

